From d46f4fdbbd34d2ff5778ba1eb8f87461cc99df6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Liszt <liszt@localhost>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 00:08:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] intake: one step is one adversary move, and warn when
 steps share a source

The import and new-scenario prompts asked for 4-7 steps while the schema caps
attack_path at 6, and they described attack_path[].layer with the same words as
ai_infrastructure_layer. Models filled the step layer with the canonical stack
strings, which are 19 and 26 characters against an 18-character limit, so records
were rejected after the fact.

Both prompts now state the ceiling as 3 to 6, give the step layer its own
vocabulary of short seam tags with worked examples, and say to tag where a move
operates rather than who performs it. They also carry the granularity rule: one
step is one adversary move that produces its own observable, internal control
flow of a single request is one step, and a control that fires is a telemetry row
rather than an attack step.

validate.py gains the matching check. When several attack-step rows name the same
source the chain has usually been split into the internals of one move, which
makes a single control look like coverage of several steps and inflates every
denominator the record feeds. It warns rather than errors, since the same source
can legitimately answer more than one move, but it asks for that to be said in
mapping_notes.
